WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
Barker Bill's Trick Shooting.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:03.900
Barker Bill is your host.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.420
Join him and Assistant Trixie for Arcade Style Trick Shooting.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:29.860
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.020
every NES game released in North America.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.020
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:40.680
And I'm Joe. And does it really count if the back of the box just uses all the same words as the full title of the game?

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.600
Barker Bill's Trick Shooting.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.000
It had Barker Bill and it had Trick Shooting and then just a bunch of words in between that.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.420
Maybe that's accidentally the front of the box.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.040
Right, right. Can you tell which one's the back of the box at this point?

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.660
There's small print in between Barker Bill's is your host.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.220
Join him and Assistant Trixie for Arcade Style Trick Shooting.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.540
Like, you just don't notice it on the front of the box.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:05.160
Oh, I agree. Her name's Trixie, like, how you shoot the gun.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:06.640
Yeah. There you go.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.380
Speaking of, thank you, Sean.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.320
This is a Zapper game.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:15.580
Why did Nintendo release another Zapper game so far away from all the others?

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:21.960
Uh, if you recall, I mean, Nintendo official ones like Duck Hunt and Hogan's Alley.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:30.820
Those games, uh, were like the height of both the launch of the NES and the beginning of the NES and nowhere near Barker Bill's release.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:37.860
By this point, the NES is kind of like getting ready to be taken over by the Super Nintendo next year.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:47.960
And the Zapper was more of like a, I don't want to call it a novelty item at this time, but we're certainly just getting, uh, you know, a release a year at this point.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.080
So, are you surprised that we came back?

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:56.280
I'm pleasantly surprised, but I don't know why they, I mean, could it have been like a, a farewell and homage?

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.940
Maybe one last attempt to get people to buy some Zappers before they move on?

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:04.720
Well, is this a Nintendo, like, developed product?

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.480
My understanding was yes.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:11.580
Um, I could check in on that for you, but, um, to Joe Duck Hunt Dog.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:12.780
Is that him though?

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.980
So this is how I was looking at it.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:26.520
Well, it makes the sound. I was looking at this game as like a third party, kinda like cash grab kinda title, where it kinda gave you like a legally distinct Duck Hunt Dog.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:27.380
Chase's it.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.200
And, uh, maybe, maybe I'm wrong though.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.100
I, I didn't do, I didn't do any research for this.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.840
It is, it is Nintendo R&D 1.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:39.720
Wow. So, uh, we can, we must assume that that is the Duck Hunt Dog.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.600
Um, I will admit, he looks like he lost some weight maybe or something.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:44.840
He's on a diet these days.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.440
I think he's been smoking.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:52.160
Like, the thing is that this whole game kinda feels like a seedier version of Duck Hunt.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.360
Like, you got, you got the dog.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:59.240
You see that many ducks murdered before your eyes and you know, you're, you, you, you start smoking.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:05.380
You, you got the dog, uh, you got Trixie who's a very scantily clad like Playboy Bunny.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:12.240
Uh, I think that this Mario lookalike is like a, is a heavy tobacco user.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:14.080
Um, I don't know.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.520
That's just sort of the vibe I was getting.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:24.100
And, and that's a good point because Duck Hunt was one of those zapper games that did more than just, uh, like one particular minigame.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.760
It had a couple different modes for a couple different kinds of trick shooting.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:29.400
Barker Bill's doing that again.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:36.440
Obviously it's right there in the title, but there's, it's not just, hey, uh, shoot the criminal instead of the police officer.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.180
Like, you know, and then that's the whole game like Hogan's alley was instead.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.400
There's a couple different minigames here.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:57.760
Uh, just wanted to touch one thing on, uh, that Joe might've, uh, implied or thought, you know, what's interesting, Joe, is that they didn't bundle this with like the zapper again to try to like sell more of them as if they had like some kind of reserve stock.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.540
There was a NES bundle with a zapper and a controller.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:18.500
And I think there was even like another set that came with the power pad too, but this particular game just kind of released, not with a zapper bundle, not with an NES console bundle, not like as a new version of the family entertainment system.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:20.880
Um, just, just here we are Nintendo.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:30.300
Nintendo, I feel, it almost feels like they forgot to release this or we're working on it while the zapper was still big to had other priorities and then said, well, we can still release this.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:31.420
Like it is finished.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:32.540
Well, all right then.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:33.960
I mean, I'm just spitballing.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:41.640
We we've had other, we've had other zapper games, not, not published by Nintendo since those, those first four.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.080
Have they done well enough?

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.640
Or maybe Nintendo was like, Hey, we, these are doing well.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:53.860
Like there's some popularity to, you know, freedom force or to the earth or whatever that like, maybe we should make another one.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.480
There's enough people buying these games.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:02.160
I don't know. If I, if I had to guess it's, it would be more like, not so much like, Oh, there's more money to be made here.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:12.620
I think it, I'm kind of applying some like psychology to it of like, well, uh, it's not really working, but we can't just pretend that we gave up.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.860
Uh, we have to support it for longer.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:18.680
So they'll get, they're giving us this token game.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:19.880
I don't know. Barker bills.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:35.220
Trick shooting is, uh, it's not, I described it as several mini games, but really it, it is just several shooting galleries, different things to shoot at, uh, balloons, plates, uh, tin cans, flying saucers.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:40.660
At the end of the day, you really are just making sure that the gun targets different things.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:47.240
It's not really, uh, it's not changing necessarily how, like the objective of each one.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:06:02.460
So while we could talk about, uh, you know, the various different stages, and I think we should at least a little bit, ultimately they disguised a way to make it seem like you're getting potentially more game than you really, uh, you know, wound up ultimately playing.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:14.500
Yeah, I think there's, there's a bit more of a difference in terms of like how you're playing the game than, uh, like it's not just a, uh, an asset swap where you're shooting different things.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.220
Like there's different ways that you're playing these mini games.

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:21.260
It, it's testing, it's challenging you at different aspects, I think.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:29.960
Yeah, I agree. And at least for two of them, there's like, there's something distinct and unique about, uh, I will say that the, uh, what is it?

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.160
The glass shooting thing or whatever, I just, I don't get that one exactly.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.880
Like it just doesn't do it for me.

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:41.880
But the other two, I did think like, okay, these are different things that we haven't really necessarily, uh, experienced.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:52.340
I mean, I guess, you know, the balloon one's not like super, super distinct from Duck Hunt, but the flying saucer is, um, has a very unique, it's hard to even say it's like unique gameplay style.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.540
It really just has to do with the way the objects are moving.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:55.720
It's a scoring system, yeah.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:05.720
Yeah. So just to run through them, uh, even though the objective is always the same, uh, accurately shoot your targets, you get the most points and you move on.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:12.400
And, uh, there's always also forbidden objects or, uh, rather like, uh, negative point totals.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.980
If you shoot, for instance, we're talking about the Duck Hunt dog.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:16.660
Yep. Don't hit him.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:18.720
I agree. You can though.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:25.780
That's, I think, my main, my main concern with the original Duck Hunt was you couldn't shoot the dog, but now you can.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:31.040
I think that, like, there's a lot of value in that, just giving you the freedom to shoot the dog.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:34.840
See, I look at it, the dog gets like angry now, you know, he's like.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:40.080
Yeah. Going back to like it being like a seedier game, like this, like suddenly this dog is like, he's pissed.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:43.780
What was the dog even laughing about in Duck Hunt?

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:45.860
Like, the fact that you missed the ducks?

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.260
No, no, yeah, but I'm saying like, what's so funny?

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:55.020
Like, wasn't he like potentially, like, weren't you his owner and that was like how you were gonna like eat a meal tonight?

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:56.940
He's obviously the alpha.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:01.700
Like, if we went shooting together and you guys missed everything, I'd laugh at you.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:03.600
Because you're the alpha. Like a reverse situation?

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:07.000
No, well, I'm just saying that the dog has very little respect for you.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:12.540
Right, okay. The different shooting galleries have some pretty fun names to them.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:19.340
We have the Balloon Saloon, which is the one that basically the duck hunt dog is in.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:22.680
And a bunch of balloons kind of appear from behind the stand.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:25.800
And you have to shoot those balloons and not all the other.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:28.580
What do we want to call those things?

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.340
Negative forbidden objects?

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:31.760
Yeah, whatever. You can't hit those things.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.220
Just a bird, right?

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:35.220
Wait, in the balloon?

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:36.100
Oh, no, no, I'm sorry.

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:41.200
No, the duck hunt dog is the only thing you shouldn't shoot in Balloon Fight.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:45.840
Okay, but what are we calling, like, the things you shouldn't, forbidden objects?

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:49.540
I mean, that's a weird way to say it, but we can go with it.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:53.740
Yeah. The dog is now categorized in the forbidden object zone.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:09:02.700
And so basically, the only way that this gets harder is just that the balloons start, like, rising faster.

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:05.140
Yeah, they behave more erratically.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:09.180
Yeah. Yeah, there's a lot of entropy to these balloons.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:21.040
I guess the closest thing that we've played before are the ducks in duck hunt, but these balloons are much more erratic, like you say, than the ducks, I believe.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:25.420
Now, Flying Saucer, I like that in that one.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:31.280
So basically, it's just shoot, they throw plates, they're throwing two plates from either side of the screen.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:36.680
And it's not just shoot the plates, because that goes without saying.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:44.360
It's also, you know, you get the most points if you kind of, like, wait for the plate to fall a bit more.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:46.780
Or you get it, like, right out of their hand.

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:53.180
So you're, like, encouraged to take some chances with your accuracy there.

00:09:53.180 --> 00:10:04.100
But that, like, at least differentiates it a bit from the duck hunt appropriate saloon balloon, balloon saloon.

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:11.560
And I think even, like, isn't it, is there any scoring difference in skeet from duck hunt?

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:13.760
I don't think so, now that you mention it.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:18.780
I think that no matter how far away the clay pigeon gets, you get the same amount of points.

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:20.540
Don't quote me. But don't shoot the bird.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:21.660
That's the forbidden object.

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:23.460
Right. Sometimes the bird will pick up the plate.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:26.980
That's true. Making it even harder. You gotta shoot it out from its feet without hitting the bird.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:34.340
It's interesting that the highest point total occurs, you know, like, near the end of the, like, when it's about to crash.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:39.000
Which I think is the correct way to give out the points because that is, like, the most risky.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:41.880
But you would think it would be, like, the highest point, right?

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:46.800
Like, you would want it to be almost like an archery thing where you're trying to hit the bullseye.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:49.040
You want to have it hit the highest point of the arc.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:54.460
Obviously, that would mean that it would have to have different arcs and not just, like, somewhere where you could park your gun.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.160
Yeah, that's kind of the reason.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:00.760
How much is that? But also, I feel like the highest point, wouldn't that be the easiest point?

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:02.240
Yeah, because it's not really moving up there.

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:08.020
Yeah, and then you get to get the most, the highest score at the easiest point feels weird to me.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:14.200
But I do think you get an actually higher score even than when it's, like, right near the ground if you hit both places at once.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:18.460
Yes. Because I think, like, you get 300 points if you hit it, like, really close to the bottom.

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:20.900
But you get 1,000 points if you hit two at once.

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:22.260
So you get, like, a two-for-one deal.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:24.780
And that's interesting because that's a timing thing, too.

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:28.980
So it's not necessarily, like, they're not throwing two plates at the same time.

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:32.740
You have to kind of hope that that happens and then seize your opportunity.

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:36.120
Right. And it could be dangerous because if you wait, you know, you might want to hit.

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:38.720
I mean, it's the same danger that you have with the rest of the game, too.

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:43.680
But, like, if you're waiting for that moment, you might miss good opportunities to shoot the plate before that moment.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:44.720
And it might never come.

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:48.460
You may just be waiting there forever.

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:51.420
Yeah. Yeah, we don't even know if this can happen.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:54.700
We're making this up. So that was, so let's see.

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:58.960
You had, like, what could have been, like, at a carnival, right, with a bunch of balloons.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:05.640
We've all seen balloon games at a carnival where you throw darts or you fill up a thing with water to pop a balloon.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:07.320
We've all seen that.

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:12.260
The flying saucer thing with the plates, again, feels kind of circusy or whatever.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:17.420
Not necessarily that they would let people do that, but that you might see an activity like that, like a sharpshooter.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:27.360
The window panes one, not sure, like, what, you know, what's the setting here that, like, okay, we're going to open up, like, some of the windows in this big building.

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:30.000
And then other ones are going to remain closed.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:34.860
And you have to only shoot when the object that's fallen is in an open window.

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:39.220
And it's not like they're that sporadically, like, opened and closed.

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:47.520
Just kind of, like, you see it dangle and then you just have to wait for it to be obviously in the other, in the open zone instead of the closed zone.

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:52.920
This is the one that I can see where this could go that would make it interesting or hard.

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:59.680
But the way that it is actually laid out, really the glass panes are just not important because just don't shoot when they're there.

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:03.680
They give you plenty of time when they're not, when it's not behind the glass pane.

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:08.760
So I just don't know what this is really, what kind of challenge this is bringing that's different than the other ones.

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:12.980
It just feels, like, the glass panes could not be there and it really wouldn't be that much different of a game.

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:14.640
I would just be waiting less often.

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:29.640
So basically what I think they're doing here, they're, so the glass panes could be anything, but depending on how much open space is in each vertical, because it doesn't really matter what's going on side to side.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:34.660
Um, you could take the easy route and just shoot it as soon as you see it.

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:37.320
And then, like, if you miss, you have more opportunities to hit.

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:43.980
Um, or you could wait until it hits the very bottom into that last open pane and get the most points.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:47.820
Um, but if you miss, you miss and you lose a life.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:51.460
Um, or you, you lose a, I don't know what you call it.

00:13:51.500 --> 00:14:04.420
I think it just lives. So I, and I mean, yeah, you could say that it's not that difficult because maybe they're going too slow, but I, as you keep playing, I think like the pain pattern state changes, uh, they get narrower.

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:08.800
The, it, it shifts around and I think things go faster.

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:16.140
I played like the next, the next mode is like a, like sort of a round, a world tour of all of them.

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:18.020
And it just kind of loops until you lose.

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:22.680
So, um, I, I want to say that that increases in difficulty there.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:30.060
So I get the concept and it's still, at least it's not just duck hunt again, but it's the least inspired of the three, I think.

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:36.740
Yeah, they do fall faster.

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:52.700
Sure. And I, I think the one idea that, that this game was onto in terms of changing up what you're shooting and how, and what happens when you shoot something is that occasionally sometimes you'll hit an item and it will then ricochet into the next column of window panes.

00:14:52.700 --> 00:15:05.320
And so, you know, it, it, it kind of like has an invulnerability for a little bit, dives deeper, lower to the ground, and then you have to shoot it again at a moment where, um, you know, there's no window panes covering it.

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:11.860
I think that could have had something if like, you have to keep tracking multiple objects as they're falling.

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:15.660
Instead, it's the, it's just one individual object at a time.

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:22.980
And the fact that they don't kind of juggle around the screen consistently, that's what, uh, loses me for the variety.

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:36.660
I think they had an interesting mechanic and they just didn't take it far enough to the point where it gets more challenging because it happens faster, but not necessarily because, uh, of your, um, of anything other than your reaction time.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:47.080
Yeah. I mean, I will admit that, uh, until we started talking about this, I actually, for some reason, didn't realize that you are getting more points the lower the items get, which seems obvious to me now.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:57.660
That makes a little bit of a difference, but, but yeah, I mean, I, I do wish that, that there was like, I wish that as the levels went on, there were like levels where it's mostly glass panes and only a few windows of open space.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:15:59.980
So you got to really think about where it is in it.

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:03.500
And at the same time, like you're saying, Mike have multiple items on the screen at once.

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:11.000
You have to kind of like decide, okay, I want to be shooting over here now, but I got to be ready to shoot over there when this one gets to it's open space or, or something like that.

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:13.880
I don't know. This one just felt the most boring and the easiest to me.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:27.440
I agree. And then John, you mentioned the final, uh, option fun follies, uh, which, which is kind of just a bonus mode after you, uh, play all the other ones and it, it has them all in it.

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:39.380
It kind of reminds me of like an early idea for a, uh, like WarioWare style game now where, you know, you're switching constantly between the different like little mini games that they have.

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:40.960
And it just keeps going.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:43.080
You keep getting harder, going faster.

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.140
You have to be more precise with the shooting.

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:51.320
Obviously WarioWare takes that to a whole nother level where like every mini game is just 10 seconds long and you just keep moving.

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:55.160
And, uh, the whole like beats per minute gets faster and faster.

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:59.360
It's not happening here, but it is like the early groundwork for something like that.

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:00.360
Yeah, I can see that.

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:13.220
